# nodejs and npm are the archive's (trixie: Node 20): Debian ships no Node 26, and the build takes no Node from outside
# the archive. Node runs once, in install-wasm's npm ci and npm pack (the pinned TypeScript's tsc, which needs >= 14.17);
# debian/rules turns engine-strict off, so ts/package.json's engines >= 26 is npm ci's warning here, not a failure.
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13), libomx-contract-dev, pkgconf, libjack-jackd2-dev, jackd2, clang, lld, wasi-libc, nodejs, npm
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://github.com/Open-Mixer/omx-dsp
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: libomxdsp-dev
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: same
Depends: libomx-contract-dev, omx-dsp-contract (= ${source:Version}), ${misc:Depends}
Description: audio toolbox of the OpenMixer console, a real-time-safe C library
 libomxdsp is the sound of the OpenMixer console, as a library: the filters,
 envelopes, delays, limiters, gates, compressors and other building blocks of
 a mixer channel, written in plain C that never allocates, locks or calls the
 system while audio is running. The console and the omx plugins are built from
 this same code, so an effect sounds the same on the desk and in your DAW,
 sample for sample. Link it into your own plugin, effect or audio tool and
 start from parts that are already tested against exact results at every
 sample rate.
 .
 This package holds the headers and the static library in three flavours,
 each with its pkg-config file: libomxdsp.a (omxdsp), libomxdsp-contracts.a
 (omxdsp-contracts, contracts compiled in) and libomxdsp-tsan.a (omxdsp-tsan,
 contracts and ThreadSanitizer). Programs link the library statically; no
 shared library exists.
 .
 Its documentation is on openmixer.org.

Package: omx-rt-gate
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: libomxdsp-dev (= ${binary:Version}), gcc, ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: static real-time gate for plugin and engine C built on libomxdsp
 omx-rt-gate proves that the real-time code of a plugin or an audio engine
 never allocates, locks, sleeps, does I/O or logs. It compiles each real-time
 source with the project's own flags, reads the compiler's call graph, finds
 the functions a host calls from its audio thread in the plugin's LV2, CLAP,
 PipeWire or JACK tables, and follows every call from there, through
 libomxdsp's own code too. Each finding names the call, its place and the path
 from the entry point. The tables it judges against ship with it: one
 forbidden list, one class table and the thread roles of each ABI, with
 libomxdsp's thread tags and call graphs.
 .
 Its documentation is on openmixer.org.

Package: omx-rta-read
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: spectrum of a JACK signal, read and never fed
 omx-rta-read reads the spectrum of a processor's signal in a JACK graph,
 what it is given and what it gives, and writes it as log-spaced bands, one
 JSON line ten times a second, for a panel to draw. It is a JACK client with
 input ports only, connected only from the ports it watches, so it never
 feeds the mix. It runs only while something reads it. The window, the
 bands and their gates are the OpenMixer console's, from libomxdsp.
 .
 Its documentation is on openmixer.org.

Package: omx-dsp-contract
Architecture: all
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: omx-contract-data, ${misc:Depends}
Description: omx-dsp's kernel and instance declarations, read at run time
 omx-dsp's package of omx-contract's contract directory: the declarations of
 its kernels and instance faces (their controls, units and readouts) under
 /usr/share/omx-contract/contract.d/3/omx-dsp. A program that reads them while
 it runs (a panel, the omx-contract command) needs this package, not the
 development one.
 .
 Its documentation is on openmixer.org.

Package: omx-dsp-wasm
Architecture: all
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: omx-dsp's curve function as a WebAssembly module, with its JavaScript loader
 The curve function of omx-dsp (the points of an EQ or transfer curve from a
 panel description's terms) compiled to one WebAssembly module,
 /usr/share/omx-dsp/wasm/omx-dsp.wasm, and the loader that loads it, beside it
 under /usr/share/omx-dsp/: dist/index.js, the files the npm package
 @openmixer/omx-dsp publishes (package.json, dist/, src/, oracle/, wasm/), the
 same files. A Node program imports /usr/share/omx-dsp/dist/index.js and calls
 loadCurve on the module; a browser bundles the same files. The arithmetic is
 the console's and the plugins', not a second implementation of it.
 .
 Its documentation is on openmixer.org.
